1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,200
In Breath of the Wild, grass catches fire,

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:03,760
and rain puts it out.

3
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:07,080
Were these combinations authored separately for every scene?

4
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:10,760
Motion calculations and state rules such as ignition are distinct.

5
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:13,200
Watch this patch of grass catch fire.

6
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:15,460
The system that handles these shared state changes

7
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:16,620
is the Chemistry Engine.

8
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:19,580
Physics calculates the motion of objects.

9
00:00:19,580 --> 00:00:21,420
The chemistry engine handles burning

10
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:23,280
and extinguished states.

11
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:24,500
The state system distinguishes

12
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:26,740
elements from materials.

13
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:28,940
Elements such as fire and materials such as wood

14
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:31,280
are assigned relationships.

15
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:33,060
Although it is called a chemistry engine,

16
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:36,400
it does not simulate all real-world chemistry.

17
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:40,020
The first rule: an element changes a material’s state.

18
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:43,260
Fire touching wood changes it into a burning state.

19
00:00:43,260 --> 00:00:46,040
A shared rule can apply in many locations.

20
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:48,500
The actual footage also shows ice

21
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:50,380
shrinking near a fire.

22
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:51,900
Distinguish pushing an object to a new position

23
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:55,000
from changing its state through heat.

24
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:55,640
The second rule:

25
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:59,180
elements can also change other elements’ states.

26
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:00,240
The relationship between water and fire

27
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:02,000
is handled within the same framework.

28
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Beyond checking only burning objects,

29
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,260
effects themselves can interact.

30
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:09,540
In the explanatory model,

31
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:11,120
bring water into contact with the fire.

32
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:12,000
The fire effect itself

33
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:13,920
is affected by another effect.

34
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,980
The third rule concerns relationships between materials.

35
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:21,160
Within these chemistry rules, they do not directly change each other’s states.

36
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:23,540
That does not mean they cannot collide.

37
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:27,900
This distinguishes motion physics from the rules for state changes.

38
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:29,780
Two wooden boxes can collide and move

39
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:32,120
without setting one another on fire.

40
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:33,480
Being pushed by a collision

41
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:36,640
and changing a chemical state are different layers.

42
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:38,020
Treat rain as the water element,

43
00:01:38,020 --> 00:01:41,420
and the existing water–fire relationship can be reused.

44
00:01:41,420 --> 00:01:42,240
Electricity and wind also belong

45
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:45,220
within the game’s simplified state model.

46
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:47,560
As more rules can be combined,

47
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:50,700
players gain more combinations to try.

48
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:52,180
The same relationship between fire and water

49
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:55,320
can be reused in a different patch of grass.

50
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:57,540
Instead of scripting a separate result for each location,

51
00:01:57,540 --> 00:02:00,760
define relationships between materials and effects.

52
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:02,060
Use physics for movement

53
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:04,940
and shared rules for state changes.

54
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:07,160
Setting a fire and extinguishing it with rain

55
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:09,620
feel like consistent rules of one world.

56
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:12,540
The goal is more than producing many events:

57
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:15,360
create relationships that can be recombined.

58
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:17,600
In short, physics handles motion;

59
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:19,440
chemistry handles state changes.
